  "from_addr": "Mike Allen <mike@axios.com>",
  "subject": "🍮 Axios Finish Line: Pudding lessons",
  "date": "Mon, 05 Oct 2026 21:29:23 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🏞️ Plus: Park du jour | Monday, October 05, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Oct 05, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Tonight's guest host is Bradley Olson, who recently joined Axios from The Wall Street Journal to lead our tech coverage. Brad writes about \"aha\" moments that've changed the way he thinks about AI.\n\n Smart Brevity™ count: 734 words … 3 mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: What boiled egg pudding says about AI \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Bradley Olson, Axios managing editor for AI &amp; tech, writes: \n\n I won't lie: The mix of food in my blender looked really gross. It was literal AI slop.\n\n I was in the middle of another AI experiment, one of many that have given me a handful of lightbulb moments with the technology that I was profoundly skeptical about only a year ago. Why it matters: To understand AI's transformational power, I had to try it, to noodle and experiment, to see if it could become useful — or even essential.\n\n Once I did, doubt turned into cautious belief — or dare I say, faith. 📱 Zoom in: What we'll call the parable of the boiled egg pudding began with a \"what if.\"\n\n Could Muse, Meta's new viral assistant , take the hundreds of high-protein recipe videos I had saved on Instagram Reels (I have a scrolling problem — don't judge! ) and turn them into a tidy recipe book, with nutrition facts included? Sure enough, it took Muse a few seconds to produce \"My Recipe Book: 250 recipes from my Instagram saved collection / Collected over time, cooked with love.\"\n\n I left the title untouched — a reminder that AI still can't do everything, even if the instant PDF was impressive. As an always-aspiring healthy eater, I had apparently saved 250 videos with plans of making them someday, but had never revisited a single one. 🍳 The intrigue: Reels is a great medium for finding recipes, not so much for following them. You have to watch the video over and over to log the ingredients, and scroll back and forth to make sure you've heard the steps right. The book made the videos instantly usable.\n\n Back to my blender: Once combined, the ingredients — six boiled eggs, cacao powder, maple syrup and some milk — turned into a pretty smooth-looking chocolate pudding. It actually tasted pretty good — not like crème brûlée or anything, but a decent solution to my never-ending late-night sweet tooth. 💡 Zoom out: The surprise of that moment, and the accompanying musings on how long it'll be before we are all living in the movie Her , reminded me of my foundational conversion moment as an AI user nearly a year ago.\n\n I had enjoyed the chatbot era, was an early power user of Google's NotebookLM, and spent many a night working on Studio Ghibli-esque portraits of my family. I liked it. But the singularity ? Give me a break, I told myself. Then my Aunt Betty died. A fiery, accomplished psychologist with a penchant for quicksilver jokes and asides, Betty passed away after a long battle with multiple sclerosis at the age of 93.\n\n I flew to New York to help her wife Marilyn (my aunt and my mother's sister) manage the aftermath. As many people are after a family death, we were both overwhelmed. Marilyn was shell-shocked. We needed to notify friends, organize a service and deal with all the end-of-life to-dos.\n\n Marilyn, a writer who had recently self-published a memoir about her pioneering, 55-year relationship with Betty, didn't feel up to the task of writing an obituary. But she also didn't want to send out a note about her wife's death without it. 🕯️ I had an idea: What if I uploaded Marilyn's memoir into NotebookLM — an AI productivity tool that synthesizes only data you compile into a notebook — and asked it to use the information to write an obituary?\n\n I provided a few prompts: Write an obituary of about 1,200 words that highlights Betty's work as an activist, her love for Marilyn, and her tendency for blunt and hilarious quips and one-liners. In a few seconds, the \"thinking machine\" produced a lovely obituary that felt very much like it was in Marilyn's voice.\n\n We then worked through it together over about an hour, with her suggesting revisions, additions and finishing touches. We read it aloud together, laughing and crying. Share this story . \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n See what's next for media in 2027 \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Media Trends Executive Annual members receive the 2026 Annual Report, our special media document featuring global forecasts, sector analysis and exclusive media data insights.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n🔒 Get the report by becoming a Media Trends Executive member.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🥾 Parting shot! \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: Joe Capraro Finish Line readers and newlyweds Madi and Joe Capraro honeymooned through national parks last month.\n\n Here's one of their stunning captures: an elk at Mammoth Hot Springs inside Yellowstone National Park in Wyoming. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
